WEBVTT

00:01.200 --> 00:03.634
Every robot policy gets a score.

00:03.704 --> 00:06.228
Run it again, and the score moves.

00:06.428 --> 00:12.844
Shift a cup a few centimeters, and success can fall from ninety-five percent to as low as fifty.

00:13.094 --> 00:15.334
The noise has two sources.

00:15.334 --> 00:16.987
Where the objects started.

00:16.987 --> 00:22.566
And who scored it. On a towel fold, judges disagree by thirty percent.

00:22.716 --> 00:25.745
Together, about ninety percent of the variance.

00:25.945 --> 00:29.920
One episode in five is invalid before the math starts.

00:30.220 --> 00:34.242
Software solved this with seeds, version control, and CI.

00:34.242 --> 00:36.871
Physical AI never had that layer.

00:37.271 --> 00:39.355
Prism is that layer.

00:39.705 --> 00:43.243
One platform, from an empty table to an answer.

00:43.343 --> 00:51.976
Define the test once. The objects, their exact poses, the rubric, and what counts as success. Every case is versioned.

00:52.076 --> 00:57.069
Assign runs to stations, and operators see exactly what to set up next.

00:57.169 --> 01:02.889
Stage. Light shows each object's pose, and guides the operator step by step.

01:02.989 --> 01:07.582
Verify. Our vision model checks every object before the trial runs.

01:07.682 --> 01:16.025
Run. Your robot, your policy. The light goes dark, so nothing leaks into training data, and Prism tracks the scene live.

01:16.075 --> 01:22.890
Score. The same model watches the trial, describes exactly what happened, and judges it against your rubric.

01:22.990 --> 01:27.478
Every score comes with a reason, and any call can go to human review.

01:27.528 --> 01:32.962
Log. Every episode keeps its video, where everything began, and why it scored.

01:33.012 --> 01:37.750
Replay. Rebuild any condition, exactly, for the next checkpoint.

01:38.050 --> 01:41.665
A seed, and a version history, for the state of a table.

01:41.665 --> 01:43.826
It all lands in the console.

01:43.976 --> 01:50.646
Compare policies head to head, see the pass rate at every start position, and catch regressions before they ship.

01:50.896 --> 02:00.289
Change one condition at a time. Position, rotation, clutter, objects, light. See exactly where a policy holds, and where it breaks.

02:00.439 --> 02:05.218
Today, twenty stations across robotics labs report to one console.

02:05.268 --> 02:10.338
At one design-partner lab, score variance fell from forty percent to ten.

02:10.638 --> 02:14.617
Thirty controlled trials now tell you what three hundred used to.

02:14.667 --> 02:21.483
It mounts above the tables you already have, works with any arm and any policy, and never touches the robot.

02:21.583 --> 02:26.798
Claim it with a code, it calibrates itself, and you're scoring trials the same afternoon.

02:27.098 --> 02:35.077
Bring your tasks and your checkpoints. Trigger scenes, stream runs in, and pull results out, through one API and SDK.

02:35.527 --> 02:39.834
Results that hold, after the person who ran them leaves the room.

02:40.384 --> 02:42.513
Prism. By The Light Company.
